    "Observation": {
      "additionalProperties": true,
      "description": "One atomic thing that was said or happened, kept verbatim in meaning.\n\nFacts hold the belief state: what is true now, deduplicated under a key.\nThat layer deliberately discards incidental detail (\"the hike took two\nhours\", \"she hid the bone in a slipper\") because it is neither stable nor\nkeyable. But that incidental detail is exactly what a single-hop recall\nquestion asks for. An observation is a self-contained, third-person\nstatement of one such detail, with relative dates already resolved, so it\ncan be matched by a later query and injected on its own.\n\nIt is append-only and never keyed: there is no belief to overwrite, only a\nrecord of what was observed. Retrieval, not reconciliation, decides what\nsurfaces. This is the portable form of the \"atomic fact\" memory that wins\non long-conversation recall benchmarks, carrying no embedding and no vector\nstore: a plain string ranked by the same pure-Python BM25 as everything\nelse.",
      "properties": {
        "evidence": {
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/EvidenceRef"
          },
          "title": "Evidence",
          "type": "array"
        },
        "source_ids": {
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "title": "Source Ids",
          "type": "array"
        },
        "stamp_ids": {
          "items": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "title": "Stamp Ids",
          "type": "array"
        },
        "author": {
          "default": "",
          "title": "Author",
          "type": "string"
        },
